#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE EIGHTH STAGE

When they had left the shepherds, they quickly came to the place where Christian met one Turn-away, who lived in the town of Apostasy. So Mr. Great-Heart their guide now reminded them of him, saying, This is the place where Christian met one Turn-away, who carried the mark of his rebellion on his back. And this I have to say about this man: he would listen to no advice, but once he began to fall, no persuasion could stop him. When he came to the place where the cross and the sepulcher were, he met someone who told him to look there; but he gnashed his teeth, and stamped, and said he was determined to go back to his own town. Before he came to the gate, he met Evangelist, who offered to lay hands on him, to turn him back into the way; but this Turn-away resisted him, and after treating him with great contempt, he got away over the wall, and so escaped his hand.

Then they went on; and just at the place where Little-Faith was once robbed, there stood a man with his sword drawn, and his face all covered with blood. Then Mr. Great-Heart said, Who are you? The man answered, I am one whose name is Valiant-for-truth. I am a pilgrim, and I am going to the Celestial City. Now, as I was on my way, three men surrounded me, and put these three things to me: 1. Whether I would become one of them. 2. Or go back to where I came from. 3. Or die on the spot. Prov 1:11–14. To the first I answered that I had been an honest man for a long time, and so they could not expect me to cast in my lot with thieves now. Then they demanded what I would say to the second. So I told them that, as for the place I came from, if I had not found it a hardship to me, I would not have left it at all; but since I found it altogether unsuited to me, and very unprofitable for me, I left it for this way. Then they asked me what I said to the third. And I told them my life had cost far too dear for me to give it away lightly. Besides, you have no business putting things to my choice like this; so if you meddle, it is at your own peril. Then these three, namely Wild-head, Inconsiderate, and Pragmatic, drew on me, and I also drew on them. So we fell to it, one against three, for more than three hours. They have left on me, as you see, some of the marks of their valor, and they have also carried away with them some of mine. They have only just now gone; I suppose they may, as the saying is, have heard your horse dash, and so they took to flight.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But those were great odds, three against one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It is true; but few and many are nothing to him who has the truth on his side. “Though an army encamps around me,” said one, Ps 27:3, “my heart will not fear; though a war breaks out against me, I will keep my trust,” etc. Besides, he said, I have read in some records that one man has fought an army: and how many did Samson kill with the jawbone of an ass!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then the guide said, Why did you not cry out, so that someone might have come to help you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: I did, to my King, who I knew could hear me and give me unseen help, and that was enough for me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then Great-Heart said to Mr. Valiant-for-truth, You have behaved worthily; let me see your sword. So he showed it to him.]

When he had taken it in his hand and looked at it for a while, he said, Ha, it is a true Jerusalem blade.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It is. Let a man have one of these blades, with a hand to wield it and skill to use it, and he may take on an angel with it. He need not fear that it will not hold, if only he knows how to lay on. Its edge will never grow blunt. It will cut flesh and bones, and soul, and spirit, and all. Heb 4:12.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But you fought a long while; I wonder you were not worn out.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: I fought until my sword stuck to my hand; and then they were joined together as if a sword grew out of my arm; and when the blood ran through my fingers, then I fought with the most courage.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You have done well; you have resisted to the point of shedding your blood, striving against sin. You shall stay with us, and come in and go out with us; for we are your companions. Then they took him and washed his wounds, and gave him some of what they had, to refresh him; and so they went on together.]

Now, as they went on, because Mr. Great-Heart was delighted with him (for he greatly loved anyone he found to be a man of valor), and because there were some in the company who were feeble and weak, he asked him about many things; first, where he was from.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: I am from Dark-land; for I was born there, and my father and mother are still there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Dark-land! said the guide; does that not lie on the same coast as the City of Destruction?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes, it does. Now what made me come on pilgrimage was this. A man called Mr. Tell-true came into our parts, and he spread the word about what Christian had done, who went from the City of Destruction; namely, how he had forsaken his wife and children, and taken up a pilgrim’s life. It was also confidently reported how he had killed a serpent that came out to resist him on his journey, and how he got through to where he meant to go. It was also told what welcome he had at all his Lord’s lodgings, especially when he came to the gates of the Celestial City; for there, the man said, he was received with the sound of trumpets by a company of shining ones. He told also how all the bells in the city rang for joy at his reception, and what golden garments he was clothed with; with many other things that I will not stop to tell now. In a word, that man told the story of Christian and his travels in such a way that my heart fell into a burning haste to go after him; and neither father nor mother could hold me back. So I got away from them, and have come this far on my way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You came in at the gate, did you not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes, yes; for the same man also told us that it would all count for nothing unless we began this way by entering at the gate.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Look, said the guide to Christiana, the pilgrimage of your husband, and what he has gained by it, is talked of far and near.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, is this Christian’s wife?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, it is indeed; and these are his four sons too.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: What, and going on pilgrimage too?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, indeed, they are following after him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It makes my heart glad. Good man, how joyful he will be when he sees those who would not go with him entering after him all the same, in at the gates into the Celestial City!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: No doubt it will be a comfort to him; for, next to the joy of seeing himself there, it will be a joy to meet his wife and children there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: But now that you have touched on that, please let me hear your opinion about it. Some wonder whether we will know one another when we are there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Do you think they will know themselves then, or that they will rejoice to see themselves in that bliss? And if they think they will know themselves and do this, why not know others, and rejoice in their welfare also? Again, since our families are our second selves, though those ties will be dissolved there, why may we not reasonably conclude that we will be more glad to see them there than to find them missing?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Well, I see where you stand on this. Do you have anything more to ask me about how I began to come on pilgrimage?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes; were your father and mother willing for you to become a pilgrim?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Oh no; they used every means imaginable to persuade me to stay at home.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, what could they say against it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They said it was an idle life; and that if I were not inclined to sloth and laziness myself, I would never think well of a pilgrim’s life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And what else did they say?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, they told me that it was a dangerous way; in fact, the most dangerous way in the world, they said, is the one the pilgrims take.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did they show you what makes this way so dangerous?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes; and on many points.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Name some of them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They told me of the Slough of Despond, where Christian was nearly smothered. They told me that there were archers standing ready in Beelzebub-castle to shoot anyone who knocked at the Wicket-gate to get in. They also told me of the wood and the dark mountains; of the hill Difficulty; of the lions; and also of the three giants, Bloody-man, Maul, and Slay-good. They said, moreover, that a foul fiend haunted the Valley of Humiliation, and that Christian was almost robbed of his life by him. Besides, they said, you must go through the Valley of the Shadow of Death, where the hobgoblins are, where the light is darkness, where the way is full of snares, pits, traps, and nooses. They told me also of Giant Despair, of Doubting Castle, and of the ruin that the pilgrims met with there. Further, they said I must go over the Enchanted Ground, which was dangerous; and that after all this I would find a river with no bridge over it; and that this river lay between me and the Celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And was this all?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No. They also told me that this way was full of deceivers, and of people who lay in wait there to turn good men out of the path.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But how did they show that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They told me that Mr. Worldly Wiseman lay in wait there to deceive. They also said that Formality and Hypocrisy were always on the road. They said too that By-ends, Talkative, or Demas would very likely pick me up; that the Flatterer would catch me in his net; or that, with green-headed Ignorance, I would presume to go on to the gate, from which he was sent back to the hole in the side of the hill, and made to go the by-way to hell.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I assure you, this was enough to discourage you; but did they stop there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No, wait. They also told me of many who had tried that way long ago, and had gone a long way on it, to see if they could find something of the glory there that so many had talked so much about from time to time; and how they came back again, and called themselves fools for ever setting foot out of doors on that path, to the satisfaction of the whole country. And they named several who did so, such as Obstinate and Pliable, Mistrust and Timorous, Turn-away and old Atheist, and several more; some of whom, they said, had gone far to see what they could find, but not one of them had gained so much as a feather’s weight by going.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did they say anything more to discourage you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes. They told me of one Mr. Fearing, who was a pilgrim, and how he found the way so lonely that he never had an hour’s comfort on it; also that Mr. Despondency had nearly starved on it; yes, and also (which I had almost forgotten) that Christian himself, about whom there has been such a noise, after all his adventures for a celestial crown, was certainly drowned in the Black River and never went a foot further, however it was hushed up.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And did none of these things discourage you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No; they seemed like so many nothings to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: How was that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, I went on believing what Mr. Tell-true had said; and that carried me past them all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then this was your victory, even your faith.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It was. I believed, and so came out, got into the way, fought all who set themselves against me, and by believing have come to this place.]

#quote[“Who would true valor see, \
Let him come hither; \
One here will constant be, \
Come wind, come weather; \
There’s no discouragement \
Shall make him once relent \
His first avow’d intent \
To be a pilgrim.]

#quote[Whoso beset him round \
With dismal stories, \
Do but themselves confound; \
His strength the more is. \
No lion can him fright, \
He’ll with a giant fight, \
But he will have a right \
To be a pilgrim.]

#quote[Hobgoblin nor foul fiend \
Can daunt his spirit; \
He knows he at the end \
Shall life inherit. \
Then fancies fly away, \
He’ll not fear what men say; \
He’ll labor night and day \
To be a pilgrim.”]

By this time they had reached the Enchanted Ground, where the air naturally tended to make one drowsy. And that place was all overgrown with briars and thorns, except here and there, where there was an enchanted arbor; and if a man sits in one, or sleeps in one, it is a question, some say, whether he will ever rise or wake again in this world. So they went over this forest, one and all, and Mr. Great-Heart went in front, because he was the guide; and Mr. Valiant-for-truth came behind as rear-guard, for fear that some fiend, or dragon, or giant, or thief might perhaps fall upon their rear and do them harm. They went on here, each man with his sword drawn in his hand; for they knew it was a dangerous place. They also cheered one another up as well as they could. Mr. Great-Heart ordered Feeble-mind to come up after him; and Mr. Despondency was under the eye of Mr. Valiant.

Now they had not gone far when a great mist and darkness fell on them all, so that for a long while they could hardly see one another. So for some time they had to feel for one another by words; for they were not walking by sight. Anyone can see that this was nothing but hard going even for the best of them; but how much worse for the women and children, who were tender both in feet and in heart! Yet so it was that, through the encouraging words of the one who led in front and the one who brought them up behind, they managed pretty well to jog along.

The way here was also very wearisome, through dirt and sludge. Nor was there, in all this ground, so much as one inn or eating-house in which to refresh the weaker ones. So here there was grunting, and puffing, and sighing, while one tumbles over a bush, another sticks fast in the dirt, and some of the children lost their shoes in the mire; while one cries out, I am down; and another, Hello, where are you? and a third, The bushes have got such a hold on me, I do not think I can get away from them.

Then they came to an arbor, warm, and promising much refreshment to the pilgrims; for it was finely made overhead, decorated with greenery, and furnished with benches and seats. It also had a soft couch in it on which the weary might lean. This, as you can imagine, all things considered, was tempting; for the pilgrims were already beginning to be worn down by the badness of the way: but not one of them made so much as a move to stop there. Indeed, as far as I could see, they paid such constant heed to the advice of their guide, and he told them so faithfully of the dangers, and of the nature of the dangers when they were at them, that usually, when they were nearest to them, they plucked up their spirits most, and encouraged one another to deny the flesh. This arbor was called The Slothful’s Friend, and was made on purpose to lure some of the pilgrims, if it could, to take up their rest there when weary.

I saw then in my dream that they went on in this lonely ground of theirs, till they came to a place where a man is apt to lose his way. Now, though in the light their guide could tell well enough how to avoid the ways that led wrong, in the dark he was brought to a standstill. But he had in his pocket a map of all the ways leading to or from the Celestial City; so he struck a light (for he never goes without his tinder-box either), and takes a look at his book or map, which tells him to be careful in that place to turn to the right. And had he not been careful here to look at his map, they would in all probability have all been smothered in the mud; for just a little ahead of them, and that at the end of the cleanest way too, was a pit, no one knows how deep, full of nothing but mud, made there on purpose to destroy the pilgrims in.

Then I thought to myself, Who, going on pilgrimage, would not want one of these maps with him, so that when he is at a standstill he may look to see which way he must take?

Then they went on in this Enchanted Ground till they came to another arbor, built by the side of the highway. And in that arbor lay two men, whose names were Heedless and Too-bold. These two had gone this far on pilgrimage; but here, being wearied with their journey, they sat down to rest, and so fell fast asleep. When the pilgrims saw them, they stood still and shook their heads; for they knew that the sleepers were in a pitiful state. Then they discussed what to do, whether to go on and leave them sleeping, or to step over to them and try to wake them; and they decided to go to them and wake them, that is, if they could; but with this caution, namely, to take care that they themselves did not sit down or accept the comfort that arbor offered.

So they went in and spoke to the men, and called each by his name, for the guide, it seems, knew them; but there was neither voice nor answer. Then the guide shook them, and did what he could to rouse them. Then one of them said, I will pay you when I get my money. At which the guide shook his head. I will fight as long as I can hold my sword in my hand, said the other. At that, one of the children laughed.

Then Christiana said, What does this mean? The guide said, They are talking in their sleep. If you strike them, beat them, or do whatever else you like to them, they will answer you in this way; or, as one of them said long ago, when the waves of the sea beat upon him, and he slept like one lying on the mast of a ship, Prov 23:34, 35, When I awake, I will seek it yet again. You know that when men talk in their sleep they say anything; their words are not governed by either faith or reason. Their words do not hang together now, just as their going on pilgrimage and their sitting down here did not hang together before. This, then, is the harm of it: when heedless people go on pilgrimage, it is twenty to one that this is what happens to them. For this Enchanted Ground is one of the last refuges that the enemy of pilgrims has; so it is, as you see, placed almost at the end of the way, and so it stands against us with all the more advantage. For when, the enemy thinks, will these fools be so eager to sit down as when they are weary? and when so likely to be weary as when they are almost at their journey’s end? That is why, I say, the Enchanted Ground is placed so near to the land Beulah, and so near the end of their race. So let pilgrims look to themselves, so that it does not happen to them as it has to these, who, as you see, have fallen asleep, and no one can wake them.

Then the pilgrims, trembling, longed to go forward; only they begged their guide to strike a light, so that they might go the rest of their way by the help of the light of a lantern. So he struck a light, and by its help they went through the rest of this way, though the darkness was very great. 2 Pet 1:19. But the children began to be very weary, and they cried out to him who loves pilgrims to make their way more comfortable. So by the time they had gone a little further, a wind arose that drove away the fog, and the air became clearer. Yet they were not off the Enchanted Ground (far from it); only now they could see one another better, and the way in which they should walk.

Now when they were almost at the end of this ground, they noticed that a little ahead of them there was a solemn noise, as of someone deeply concerned. So they went on and looked ahead: and there, as they thought, they saw a man on his knees, with hands and eyes lifted up, speaking, as they thought, earnestly to one who was above. They drew near, but could not make out what he said; so they went softly till he had finished. When he had finished, he got up and began to run toward the Celestial City. Then Mr. Great-Heart called after him, saying, Hello there, friend, let us have your company, if you are going, as I suppose you are, to the Celestial City. So the man stopped, and they came up to him. But as soon as Mr. Honest saw him, he said, I know this man. Then Mr. Valiant-for-truth said, Please tell us, who is it? It is one, he said, who comes from near where I lived. His name is Standfast; he is certainly a truly good pilgrim.

So they came up to one another; and at once Standfast said to old Honest, Why, father Honest, are you there? Yes, he said, that I am, as sure as you are there. I am truly glad, said Mr. Standfast, that I have found you on this road. And I am just as glad, said the other, that I caught sight of you on your knees. Then Mr. Standfast blushed, and said, But why, did you see me? Yes, that I did, said the other, and my heart was glad at the sight. Why, what did you think? said Standfast. Think! said old Honest; what could I think? I thought we had an honest man on the road, and so would soon have his company. If you thought rightly, said Standfast, how happy I am! But if I am not as I should be, I alone must bear it. That is true, said the other; but your fear makes me all the more sure that things are right between the Prince of pilgrims and your soul. For he says, “Blessed is the man who fears always.” Prov 28:14.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Well, but, brother, please tell us what it was that brought you to your knees just now: was it because some special mercy had laid obligations on you, or what was it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Why, we are, as you see, on the Enchanted Ground; and as I was coming along, I was thinking to myself how dangerous the road in this place was, and how many who had come even this far on pilgrimage had been stopped and destroyed here. I thought also of the kind of death by which this place destroys men. Those who die here die of no violent disease: the death they die is not painful to them. For he who goes off in his sleep begins that journey with desire and pleasure. Indeed, such men give themselves up willingly to the will of that disease.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then Mr. Honest, interrupting him, said, Did you see the two men asleep in the arbor?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Yes, yes, I saw Heedless and Too-bold there; and for all I know, there they will lie till they rot. Prov 10:7. But let me go on with my story. As I was thinking like this, as I said, someone in very fine clothes, but old, came and presented herself to me, and offered me three things, namely, her body, her purse, and her bed. Now the truth is, I was both weary and sleepy. I am also as poor as an owlet, and perhaps the witch knew that. Well, I rebuffed her again and again, but she brushed my refusals aside and smiled. Then I began to be angry; but she did not care about that at all. Then she made her offers again, and said that if I would let her rule me, she would make me great and happy; for, she said, I am the mistress of the world, and men are made happy by me. Then I asked her name, and she told me it was Madam Bubble. This drove me further from her; but she still followed me with enticements. Then I went, as you saw, to my knees, and with hands lifted up, and cries, I prayed to Him who had said he would help. So, just as you came up, the gentlewoman went her way. Then I went on giving thanks for this great deliverance of mine; for I truly believe she meant no good, but rather was trying to stop me on my journey.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: No doubt her designs were bad. But wait, now that you talk of her, I think I have either seen her or read some story about her.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Perhaps you have done both.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Madam Bubble! Is she not a tall, handsome lady, with a rather dark complexion?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Right, you have hit it: she is just like that.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Does she not speak very smoothly, and give you a smile at the end of a sentence?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: You have hit on it again, for that is just how she behaves.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Does she not wear a great purse at her side, and is her hand not often in it, fingering her money, as if that were her heart’s delight?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: That is just it; if she had stood here all this while, you could not have described her to me more fully, nor have pictured her features better.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then he who drew her picture was a good painter, and he who wrote of her told the truth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: This woman is a witch, and it is by the power of her sorceries that this ground is enchanted. Whoever lays his head down in her lap might as well lay it on the block over which the axe hangs; and whoever sets eyes on her beauty is counted an enemy of God. It is she who keeps in their splendor all those who are the enemies of pilgrims. Jas 4:4. Indeed, it is she who has bought many a man off from a pilgrim’s life. She is a great gossip; she is always, she and her daughters, at one pilgrim’s heels or another’s, now commending, and then promoting the excellences of this life. She is a bold and impudent hussy: she will talk with any man. She always laughs poor pilgrims to scorn, but highly praises the rich. If there is someone clever at getting money in a place, she will speak well of him from house to house. She is extremely fond of banqueting and feasting; she is always at one full table or another. She has put it about in some places that she is a goddess, and so some worship her. She has her times and open places of cheating; and she will say and swear that no one can show a good to compare with hers. She promises to live with children’s children, if only they will love her and make much of her. In some places and to some persons she will throw gold out of her purse like dust. She loves to be sought after, spoken well of, and to lie in the bosoms of men. She is never tired of praising her goods, and she loves those most who think best of her. She will promise crowns and kingdoms to some, if only they will take her advice; yet she has brought many to the noose, and ten thousand times more to hell.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Oh, said Standfast, what a mercy it is that I resisted her; for where might she have drawn me!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Where? Indeed, none but God knows where. But in general, to be sure, she would have drawn you into many foolish and harmful lusts, which drown men in ruin and destruction. 1 Tim 6:9. It was she who set Absalom against his father, and Jeroboam against his master. It was she who persuaded Judas to sell his Lord, and who won Demas over to forsake the godly pilgrim’s life. No one can tell the harm she does. She stirs up strife between rulers and subjects, between parents and children, between neighbor and neighbor, between a man and his wife, between a man and himself, between the flesh and the spirit. So, good Mr. Standfast, be as your name is, and when you have done all, stand.]

At this talk there was a mixture of joy and trembling among the pilgrims; but at length they broke out and sang,

#quote[“What danger is the Pilgrim in! \
How many are his foes! \
How many ways there are to sin \
No living mortal knows.]

#quote[Some in the ditch are spoiled, yea, can \
Lie tumbling in the mire: \
Some, though they shun the frying-pan \
Do leap into the fire.”]

After this, I watched until they had come into the land of Beulah, where the sun shines night and day. Here, because they were weary, they gave themselves up to rest for a while. And because this country was open to pilgrims, and because the orchards and vineyards here belonged to the King of the Celestial country, they were free to help themselves to any of his things. But a little while soon refreshed them here; for the bells rang so, and the trumpets sounded so continually and so melodiously, that they could not sleep; and yet they were as refreshed as if they had slept ever so soundly. Here also the cry of all who walked the streets was, More pilgrims have come to town! And another would answer, And so many went over the water, and were let in at the golden gates today! They would cry again, There is now a legion of shining ones just come to town, by which we know that there are more pilgrims on the road; for they come here to wait for them, and to comfort them after all their sorrow. Then the pilgrims got up and walked to and fro. But how their ears were now filled with heavenly sounds, and their eyes delighted with celestial visions! In this land they heard nothing, saw nothing, felt nothing, smelled nothing, tasted nothing that was offensive to their stomach or mind; only when they tasted the water of the river they were to go over, they thought it tasted a little bitter to the palate; but it proved sweeter when it was down.

In this place a record was kept of the names of those who had been pilgrims long ago, and a history of all the famous deeds they had done. There was also much talk here of how the river had been in flood for some, and how it had ebbed while others went over. It has been almost dry for some, while it has overflowed its banks for others.

In this place the children of the town would go into the King’s gardens and gather posies for the pilgrims, and bring them to them with much affection. Here also grew henna, with spikenard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon, with all the trees of frankincense, myrrh, and aloes, with all the chief spices. With these the pilgrims’ rooms were perfumed while they stayed here; and with these their bodies were anointed, to prepare them to go over the river when the appointed time had come.

Now, while they lay here waiting for the good hour, word went round the town that a messenger had come from the Celestial City, with a matter of great importance for one Christiana, the wife of Christian the pilgrim. So inquiries were made for her, and the house where she was staying was found. So the messenger handed her a letter. It read: Hail, good woman; I bring you news that the Master calls for you, and expects you to stand in his presence in clothes of immortality within these ten days.

When he had read this letter to her, he gave her with it a sure token that he was a true messenger, and had come to tell her to make haste to be gone. The token was an arrow with a point sharpened with love, let gently into her heart, which by degrees worked so effectually in her that at the appointed time she must be gone.

When Christiana saw that her time had come, and that she was the first of this company to go over, she called for Mr. Great-Heart her guide, and told him how matters stood. So he told her he was heartily glad of the news, and would have been glad if the messenger had come for him. Then she asked him to advise her how everything should be prepared for her journey. So he told her, saying, Such and such must be done, and we who survive will go with you to the river-side.

Then she called for her children and gave them her blessing, and told them that she had read with comfort the mark that was set on their foreheads, and was glad to see them there with her, and that they had kept their garments so white. Lastly, she left to the poor the little she had, and charged her sons and daughters to be ready for when the messenger should come for them.

When she had spoken these words to her guide and to her children, she called for Mr. Valiant-for-truth, and said to him, Sir, you have shown yourself true-hearted in every place; be faithful unto death, and my King will give you a crown of life. Rev 2:10. I would also ask you to keep an eye on my children; and if at any time you see them faint, speak comforting words to them. As for my daughters, my sons’ wives, they have been faithful, and the fulfilling of the promise to them will be their end. But she gave Mr. Standfast a ring.

Then she called for old Mr. Honest, and said of him, “Here is a true Israelite, in whom there is no deceit!” John 1:47. Then he said, I wish you a fair day when you set out for Mount Zion, and I will be glad to see you go over the river dry-shod. But she answered, Come wet, come dry, I long to be gone; for however the weather is on my journey, I will have time enough when I get there to sit down and rest and dry myself.

Then that good man Mr. Ready-to-halt came in to see her. So she said to him, Your journey so far has been hard; but that will make your rest all the sweeter. Watch, and be ready; for the messenger may come at an hour when you do not expect him.

After him came Mr. Despondency and his daughter Much-afraid, to whom she said, You#super[+] ought to remember forever, with thankfulness, your#super[+] deliverance from the hands of Giant Despair and out of Doubting Castle. The fruit of that mercy is that you#super[+] have been brought here in safety. You#super[+] be watchful, and cast away fear; be sober, and hope to the end.

Then she said to Mr. Feeble-mind, You were delivered from the mouth of Giant Slay-good so that you might live in the light of the living and see your King with comfort. Only I advise you to repent of your readiness to fear and doubt his goodness before he sends for you; so that you will not be forced, when he comes, to stand before him blushing for that fault.

Now the day drew near when Christiana must go. So the road was full of people come to see her set out on her journey. But behold, all the banks beyond the river were full of horses and chariots, which had come down from above to go with her to the city gate. So she came out and entered the river, with a wave of farewell to those who followed her. The last words she was heard to say were, I come, Lord, to be with you and bless you! So her children and friends went back to their place, for those who waited for Christiana had carried her out of their sight. So she went and called, and entered in at the gate with all the ceremonies of joy with which her husband Christian had entered before her. At her departure, the children wept. But Mr. Great-Heart and Mr. Valiant played on the well-tuned cymbal and harp for joy. So all went off to their own places.

In the course of time a messenger came to the town again, and his business was with Mr. Ready-to-halt. So he sought him out and said, I have come from Him whom you have loved and followed, though on crutches; and my message is to tell you that he expects you at his table to eat supper with him in his kingdom, the day after Easter; so prepare yourself for this journey. Then he also gave him a token that he was a true messenger, saying, “I have broken your golden bowl, and loosed your silver cord.” Eccl 12:6.

After this, Mr. Ready-to-halt called for his fellow-pilgrims and told them, I am sent for, and God will surely visit you too. So he asked Mr. Valiant to make his will. And because he had nothing to bequeath to those who would survive him but his crutches and his good wishes, he said this: These crutches I bequeath to my son who will tread in my steps, with a hundred warm wishes that he may prove better than I have been.

Then he thanked Mr. Great-Heart for his guidance and kindness, and so made ready for his journey. When he came to the brink of the river, he said, Now I will have no more need of these crutches, since there are chariots and horses over there for me to ride on. The last words he was heard to say were, Welcome life! So he went his way.

After this, word was brought to Mr. Feeble-mind that the messenger had sounded his horn at the door of his room. Then he came in and told him, I have come to tell you that your Master has need of you, and that in a very little while you must behold his face in brightness. And take this as a token of the truth of my message: “Those watching through windows are darkened.” Eccl 12:3. Then Mr. Feeble-mind called for his friends, and told them what message had been brought to him, and what token he had received of the truth of it. Then he said, Since I have nothing to leave to anyone, what is the point of my making a will? As for my feeble mind, I will leave that behind me, for I will have no need of it in the place where I am going, nor is it worth giving to the poorest pilgrims: so, when I am gone, I want you, Mr. Valiant, to bury it in a dunghill. When this was done, and the day had come on which he was to depart, he entered the river like the rest. His last words were, Hold out, faith and patience! So he went over to the other side.

When many days had passed, Mr. Despondency was sent for; for a messenger had come, and brought him this message: Trembling man! this is to summon you to be ready with the King by next Lord’s day, to shout for joy for your deliverance from all your doubtings. And, said the messenger, to prove that my message is true, take this: so he gave him a grasshopper to be a burden to him. Eccl 12:5.

Now Mr. Despondency’s daughter, whose name was Much-afraid, said, when she heard what had happened, that she would go with her father. Then Mr. Despondency said to his friends, My daughter and I, you know what we have been, and how troublesome we have been in every company. My will and my daughter’s is that our desponds and slavish fears be received by no man, from the day of our departure, forever; for I know that after my death they will offer themselves to others. For, to be plain with you, they are ghosts which we took in when we first began to be pilgrims, and could never shake off afterward; and they will walk about and look for a welcome from the pilgrims: but for our sakes, shut the doors on them. When the time came for them to depart, they went up to the brink of the river. The last words of Mr. Despondency were, Farewell, night; welcome, day! His daughter went through the river singing, but no one could understand what she said.

Then a while later a messenger came to the town and asked for Mr. Honest. So he came to the house where he was, and put these lines into his hand: You are commanded to be ready a week from this day, to present yourself before your Lord at his Father’s house. And for a token that my message is true, “All the daughters of song grow faint.” Eccl 12:4. Then Mr. Honest called for his friends, and said to them, I die, but I will make no will. As for my honesty, it shall go with me; let him who comes after be told of this. When the day came for him to go, he made ready to go over the river. Now the river at that time overflowed its banks in some places; but Mr. Honest, in his lifetime, had arranged with one Good-conscience to meet him there, which he did, and gave him his hand, and so helped him over. The last words of Mr. Honest were, Grace reigns! So he left the world.

After this the news went round that Mr. Valiant-for-truth had received a summons by the same messenger as the other, and had this as a token that the summons was true, “That his pitcher was broken at the fountain.” Eccl 12:6. When he understood it, he called for his friends and told them of it. Then he said, I am going to my Father’s; and though I have got here with great difficulty, yet now I do not regret all the trouble I have gone through to arrive where I am. My sword I give to him who shall succeed me in my pilgrimage, and my courage and skill to him who can get it. My marks and scars I carry with me, to be a witness for me that I have fought His battles who will now be my rewarder. When the day came that he must go from here, many went with him to the river-side, and as he went into it he said, “Death, where is your sting?” And as he went down deeper, he said, “Grave, where is your victory?” 1 Cor 15:55. So he passed over, and all the trumpets sounded for him on the other side.

Then a summons came for Mr. Standfast. This Mr. Standfast was the one whom the rest of the pilgrims found on his knees in the Enchanted Ground. And the messenger brought it to him open in his hands; it said that he must prepare for a change of life, for his Master was not willing for him to be so far from him any longer. At this Mr. Standfast fell into deep thought. No, said the messenger, you need not doubt the truth of my message; for here is a token of its truth, “Your wheel is broken at the cistern.” Eccl 12:6. Then he called Mr. Great-Heart, who was their guide, to him and said to him, Sir, although it was not my lot to be much in your good company during the days of my pilgrimage, yet since the time I have known you, you have done me good. When I came from home, I left behind me a wife and five small children; let me ask you, when you return (for I know that you go and return to your Master’s house, in the hope that you may yet be a conductor to more of the holy pilgrims), to send to my family, and let them know all that has happened and shall happen to me. Tell them moreover of my happy arrival at this place, and of the blessed condition I am in now and have been in of late. Tell them also of Christian and Christiana his wife, and how she and her children came after her husband. Tell them also what a happy end she made, and where she has gone. I have little or nothing to send to my family, unless it is prayers and tears for them; it will be enough for you to tell them of these, if perhaps they may prevail. When Mr. Standfast had set things in order like this, and the time had come for him to hurry away, he too went down to the river. Now there was a great calm in the river at that time; so Mr. Standfast, when he was about half-way in, stood a while and talked with his companions who had gone with him there. And he said, This river has been a terror to many; yes, the thought of it has often frightened me too; but now I think I stand at ease; my foot is fixed on that on which the feet of the priests who bore the ark of the covenant stood while Israel went over Jordan. Josh 3:17. The waters indeed are bitter to the palate, and cold to the stomach; yet the thought of what I am going to, and of the convoy that waits for me on the other side, lies like a glowing coal at my heart. I see myself now at the end of my journey; my toilsome days are ended. I am going to see that head which was crowned with thorns, and that face which was spit upon for me. Until now I have lived by hearsay and faith; but now I am going where I shall live by sight, and shall be with him in whose company I delight. I have loved to hear my Lord spoken of; and wherever I have seen the print of his shoe in the earth, there I have longed to set my foot too. His name has been to me like a civet-box; yes, sweeter than all perfumes. His voice has been most sweet to me, and I have desired his face more than those who have most desired the light of the sun. I used to gather his words for my food, and as antidotes against my faintings. He has held me, and has kept me from my iniquities; yes, my steps he has strengthened in his way.

Now, while he was speaking like this, his face changed; his strong man bowed under him; and after he had said, Take me, for I come to you, they saw him no more.

But it was glorious to see how the open region was filled with horses and chariots, with trumpeters and pipers, with singers and players on stringed instruments, to welcome the pilgrims as they went up and followed one another in at the beautiful gate of the city.

As for Christiana’s children, the four boys Christiana brought with her, with their wives and children, I did not stay where I was until they had gone over. Also, since I came away, I have heard someone say that they were still alive, and would remain so for the increase of the church in the place where they were, for a time.

Should it be my lot to go that way again, I may give those who wish it an account of what I am silent about here: meanwhile I bid my reader

#align(center)[FAREWELL.]

#align(center)[THE END.]
